{"experiments":[
  {
    "hypothesis":"Theta‐band (4–7 Hz) event‐related synchronization at sentence onset is larger for longer sentences compared to shorter sentences.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Quantify and compare sentence‐onset theta power increase for long versus short sentences within each session.",
      "steps":"1. Load MEG data and the corresponding events.tsv for sessions 1–10. 2. From events of kind=='word', group by sentenceidx and identify the first word of each sentence (smallest wordidx). 3. Compute sentence duration for each group using max(timesentence). 4. Split sentences into “long” and “short” bins by median duration within each session. 5. For each sentence onset (timemeg of first word), extract MEG epochs from –0.2 s to +0.5 s. 6. Bandpass‐filter each epoch at 4–7 Hz (4th‐order Butterworth), compute analytic signal, obtain power. 7. For each epoch and channel, compute mean power in 0–0.3 s post‐onset minus baseline (–0.2 to 0). 8. Aggregate across channels by median to yield one theta‐power change per sentence. 9. Compare the long versus short sentence theta‐power changes via paired t‐test across sentences within each session or use a mixed‐effects model with sentence length bin as fixed effect and session/sentence as random effects. 10. Visualize group mean and confidence intervals for each bin.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of per‐sentence theta‐power change, sentence duration, and length bin. 2. Paired t‐test (or mixed‐effects) results: t‐statistic, p‐value, effect size. 3. Boxplot comparing theta‐power change for long vs. short sentences. 4. Report on whether longer sentences evoke significantly larger theta synchronization."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Post‐word‐onset beta‐band (13–30 Hz) desynchronization magnitude increases with word character length.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether longer words elicit larger beta‐power decreases after onset compared to shorter words.",
      "steps":"1. Load MEG and events.tsv for sessions 1–10. 2. Filter events for kind=='word', record segment string and timemeg. 3. Compute word_length = number of characters in segment. 4. Split word events into “short” and “long” groups by median word_length across all sessions. 5. For each word onset, extract MEG epoch from –0.1 to +0.4 s around timemeg. 6. Bandpass‐filter epochs at 13–30 Hz, compute Hilbert‐derived power. 7. For each epoch and channel, compute mean post‐onset power (0–0.3 s) relative to baseline (–0.1 to 0). 8. Compute percent change per epoch and channel, then median‐aggregate across channels to one value per word. 9. Compare the distributions for short vs long words with an independent‐samples t‐test (or nonparametric test). 10. Plot mean percent change and 95% CI for both groups.",
      "deliverables":"1. Dataset of word events with word_length and median beta‐change. 2. Statistical comparison: t‐statistic, p‐value, Cohen’s d. 3. Bar graph of group mean beta‐desynchronization for short and long words. 4. Interpretation whether longer words produce stronger beta desynchronization."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Gradiometer channels exhibit larger gamma‐band (30–60 Hz) event‐related power increases at phoneme onsets than magnetometer channels.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare gamma‐power response at phoneme onsets between gradiometer and magnetometer channel types.",
      "steps":"1. Load channel_types attribute and MEG/events data for sessions 1–10. 2. Identify phoneme onsets (events where kind=='phoneme') and extract timemeg. 3. Epoch MEG from –0.05 to +0.25 s around each phoneme onset. 4. Bandpass‐filter at 30–60 Hz, compute Hilbert envelope and power for each epoch/channel. 5. For each epoch and channel, compute mean power in 0–0.2 s post‐onset minus baseline (–0.05 to 0). 6. Label channels by type (grad vs mag). 7. For each epoch, average percent power change across all gradiometers and separately across magnetometers. 8. Across epochs, compare the two channel‐type mean changes with a paired t‐test. 9. Visualize time‐courses of gamma power separately for both types.",
      "deliverables":"1. Array of per‐epoch, per‐channel‐type gamma‐power changes. 2. Paired t‐test results and effect size. 3. Plot of average gamma‐power time course for grad vs mag sensors with confidence bands. 4. Conclusion on whether gradiometers show stronger gamma responses."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Across sessions, the median offset between MEG time (timemeg) and audio time (timeds) drifts linearly with session number, indicating alignment shift.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess whether the systematic difference timemeg–timeds changes across the ten recording sessions.",
      "steps":"1. Load events.tsv for sessions 1–10. 2. For each event row, compute offset = timemeg – timeds. 3. Within each session, compute the median offset across all events. 4. Plot median offset versus session index (1–10). 5. Fit a linear regression (or Spearman correlation) of median offset on session number. 6. Test slope significance (t‐test on regression coefficient or Spearman p‐value). 7. Visualize with scatter and best‐fit line plus 95% CI.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of session vs median timemeg–timeds offset. 2. Regression summary: slope, intercept, p‐value, R². 3. Scatter plot with regression line and CI. 4. Statement on the presence and direction of any drift."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The trial‐to‐trial variability (standard deviation) of alpha‐band (8–12 Hz) power during silence segments decreases as the session progresses, reflecting neural adaptation over time.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test for a negative trend in alpha power variability across successive silence segments within each session.",
      "steps":"1. Load MEG and events.tsv for sessions 1–10. 2. Identify silence segments (kind=='silence') with duration ≥0.3 s. 3. For each segment, extract a 0.3‐s window from its timemeg start. 4. Bandpass‐filter at 8–12 Hz, compute envelope power and average across channels to get one alpha power per segment. 5. Compute a rolling standard deviation of these powers over a window of 20 successive segments. 6. For each session, regress rolling std against segment‐order index. 7. Test whether the slope is significantly negative across sessions via one‐sample t‐test. 8. Plot example session’s rolling std vs segment index with regression line.",
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐segment alpha power and rolling std arrays. 2. Distribution of slopes across sessions, with mean and t‐test result. 3. Example rolling‐std time series plot with fit. 4. Conclusion on whether alpha variability declines over time."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Gamma‐band (60–100 Hz) bursts at word onsets are positively cross‐frequency coupled with theta (4–7 Hz) amplitude, indicating nested oscillatory processing.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Measure cross‐frequency coupling between theta phase and gamma amplitude during word‐onset epochs.",
      "steps":"1. Load MEG and events.tsv for sessions 1–10. 2. Filter events for kind=='word', extract timemeg for each. 3. Epoch from –0.2 to +0.5 s around each word onset. 4. Bandpass‐filter epochs at 4–7 Hz and 60–100 Hz separately. 5. For theta band, compute analytic phase; for gamma band, compute analytic amplitude. 6. For each epoch/channel, compute modulation index (e.g., Tort et al.) between theta phase and gamma amplitude in 0–0.3 s post‐onset. 7. Average MI across channels to one value per epoch. 8. Test whether MI is significantly greater than zero via one‐sample t‐test across all word epochs. 9. Visualize comodulogram across representative channels.",
      "deliverables":"1. Array of per‐epoch MI values. 2. One‐sample t‐test statistic and p‐value. 3. Example comodulogram of theta phase–gamma amplitude coupling. 4. Report on presence of significant cross‐frequency coupling at word onsets."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Sentences occurring later in a chapter evoke smaller sentence‐onset gamma‐band (30–60 Hz) power increases compared to sentences at the chapter’s start, reflecting cognitive fatigue.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare gamma ERF at sentence onset for early versus late sentences within each chapter (session).",
      "steps":"1. Load events.tsv for a given session (each file corresponds to one chapter). 2. From events (kind=='word'), group by sentenceidx and record the first word’s timemeg and timechapter. 3. Order sentences by timechapter within the session, split into early (first 50%) and late (last 50%). 4. For each sentence onset, extract MEG epochs (–0.1 to +0.4 s). 5. Bandpass‐filter epochs at 30–60 Hz, compute Hilbert amplitude power. 6. For each epoch/channel, compute mean post‐onset power (0–0.3 s) minus baseline (–0.1 to 0). 7. Median‐aggregate across channels to yield per‐sentence gamma change. 8. Compare early vs late sentence gamma changes across sentences via paired t‐test. 9. Repeat for all sessions and summarize session‐level effects.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of sentence gamma changes with early/late labels. 2. Paired t‐test results per session and aggregated. 3. Line plot of mean gamma change vs sentence order percentile. 4. Interpretation on gamma attenuation over chapter progression."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Within phoneme processing epochs, per‐channel beta (13–30 Hz) and gamma (30–60 Hz) power fluctuations are positively correlated, indicating parallel motor and perceptual processes.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess trial‐by‐trial correlation between beta and gamma power across channels during phoneme onsets.",
      "steps":"1. Load MEG data and phoneme events for sessions 1–10. 2. Extract epochs (–0.05 to +0.3 s) around timemeg for kind=='phoneme'. 3. Bandpass‐filter each epoch at 13–30 Hz and 30–60 Hz, compute Hilbert‐derived power time courses. 4. For each epoch and channel, compute mean beta and gamma power in 0–0.25 s post‐onset. 5. For each channel, compute Pearson correlation across epochs between beta and gamma mean powers. 6. Fisher‐z transform correlations and test whether mean z is >0 across channels using one‐sample t‐test. 7. Visualize the distribution of per‐channel correlations and scalp map of significant channels.",
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐channel correlation coefficients and z‐scores. 2. One‐sample t‐test on Fisher‐z scores: t, p, CI. 3. Histogram of channel‐wise correlations. 4. Scalp layout marking channels with significant positive beta–gamma coupling."
    }
  }
]}
